--- abstract

Email messages often include files as attachments, but message size limits mean
that large files are often uploaded to a web server and sent as a link
instead.  A link loses the properties that make an attachment useful: it
can stop working, the file behind it can change, it can't be scanned in
transit, and it may need a separate login.

This document defines the "Link-Attachment" header field, which
describes a file stored on a web server well enough that receiving
systems can fetch, verify and store it when the message arrives, and then
treat it like any other attachment.  It also defines markup that lets
aware clients hide the fallback links in the message body.

--- middle

# Introduction {#intro}

Most email systems limit the size of messages, sometimes to as little
as 10 megabytes, and the limits differ between systems.  So large files
are often uploaded to a web server and sent as a link instead.  Many
clients and services do this automatically, each in their own way.

An attached file has properties that a link lacks:

1. A copy lasts as long as the message.
2. It can't be changed after sending.
3. Systems that handle the message can scan it.
4. Anyone who has the message has the file.

A link can stop working, the file behind it can change, it can't be
scanned in transit, and it may need a separate login.

This document defines the "Link-Attachment" header field ({{header}}),
which gives the URL, size and digest of each file, and optionally the
key it was encrypted with.  Receiving servers fetch, verify and scan
the file when the message arrives ({{receiving}}), and keep it as long
as the message ({{lifetime}}), which restores those properties.  The
message body also includes ordinary links for clients that don't
support this document, and markup that lets clients that do hide them
({{html}}).

This document replaces {{BIG-FILES-00}}; see {{changes}}.

# Conventions and Definitions

{::boilerplate bcp14-tagged}

This document uses the terms "Mail User Agent" (MUA), "Mail Delivery
Agent" (MDA) and "Message Store" (MS) from {{MAIL-ARCH}}.  It also uses
the following terms:

linked attachment:
: A file described by a Link-Attachment header field.

attachment server:
: The HTTP {{HTTP}} server from which the sender has made a linked
  attachment available.

receiving server:
: Any system that handles a message on the recipient's side and
  inspects its content, such as an MDA, a content filter, or an MS.

# The Link-Attachment Header Field {#header}

The "Link-Attachment" header field describes one linked attachment.  A
message contains one Link-Attachment header field for each linked
attachment.

The header field appears only in the header section of the message
itself, not in the header section of a MIME body part.  A Link-Attachment
header field in a body part MUST be ignored.

The header field uses the same syntax as the Content-Disposition header
field {{CONTENT-DISP}}: a disposition type, followed by parameters.  This
lets existing MIME parsers read it.  The disposition type is
"attachment".  A receiver MUST treat an unrecognised disposition type
as "attachment", as {{CONTENT-DISP}} does.

## Parameters {#params}

Parameter names are case-insensitive.  Each parameter MUST NOT appear
more than once in a header field; a receiver MUST ignore a header field
in which a parameter defined here appears more than once.  Receivers
MUST ignore parameters they don't recognise.

Any parameter value can be encoded using {{MIME-PARAM}}, which allows
long values to be split across several lines and allows non-ASCII
characters in "filename".

cid:
: REQUIRED.  An identifier for this linked attachment, using the syntax
  of the "id-left@id-right" portion of a msg-id {{MAIL}}.  It MUST be
  unique among the Link-Attachment header fields of the message, and
  SHOULD be globally unique, in the same way as a Content-ID {{CID}}.  It
  identifies the attachment in the HTML body ({{html}}), and lets a
  forwarded or replied-to message refer to the same attachment.

url:
: REQUIRED.  An absolute "https" URI {{URI}} from which the content of
  the linked attachment can be fetched with a GET request ({{fetching}}).

size:
: REQUIRED.  The size of the file in octets, as a decimal number with
  no leading zeros.  This is the size of the file itself, after any
  decryption.

digest-*:
: At least one REQUIRED.  A digest of the file, after any decryption.
  The part of the parameter name after "digest-" is a hash algorithm
  name from the IANA "Hash Algorithms for HTTP Digest Fields" registry
  {{DIGEST-FIELDS}}, for example "digest-sha-256".  The value is the
  digest, encoded in base64 {{BASE64}} with padding.  Senders MUST
  include "digest-sha-256", and MAY include others.  Algorithms whose
  registry status is "deprecated" MUST NOT be used.

filename:
: RECOMMENDED.  A suggested file name, with the same meaning and
  handling as the "filename" parameter of Content-Disposition
  {{CONTENT-DISP}}.

type:
: OPTIONAL.  The media type of the file, as a type/subtype pair with no
  parameters.  If absent, the type is "application/octet-stream".  The
  Content-Type of the HTTP response does not override this value.

key:
: OPTIONAL.  The encryption algorithm and key with which the file was
  encrypted before it was stored on the attachment server, in the
  format defined in {{key-format}}.  If absent, the file is stored
  unencrypted.

expires:
: OPTIONAL.  The date and time, in the "date-time" syntax of {{MAIL}},
  until which the sender intends the file to be available from the URL.
  The file might remain available after this time, or vanish before it.

## Key Format {#key-format}

A key is written as the name of the encryption algorithm, a colon, and
the key material encoded in base64url {{BASE64}} without padding:

~~~ abnf
key-value       = enc-alg ":" key-material
enc-alg         = 1*( %x61-7A / DIGIT / "-" )
                  ; content coding name, in lowercase
key-material    = 1*( ALPHA / DIGIT / "-" / "_" )
                  ; base64url without padding
~~~

The algorithm is an encrypting HTTP content coding ({{Section 16.6.1
of HTTP}}).  A content coding defines the whole stored format, not just
the cipher, so a receiver can verify a large file as it streams.

The only algorithm allowed is "aes128gcm" {{ECE}}.  The key material is
the input keying material of {{Section 2.2 of ECE}}: at least 16 random
octets.  The "keyid" field SHOULD be empty.  The attachment server
serves the encrypted file without a Content-Encoding header field, and
the receiver decrypts it.

A receiver that doesn't support the algorithm MUST treat the linked
attachment as unavailable.

{:aside}
> Open issue: How should other content codings be allowed later?

## Formal Syntax {#abnf}

The following syntax uses ABNF {{ABNF}}.  Rules not defined here
("token", "quoted-string", "value", "attribute", "extension-token") are
from {{MIME}}, and CFWS, id-left, id-right and date-time are from
{{MAIL}}.

~~~ abnf
link-attachment = "Link-Attachment:" [CFWS] la-disposition
                  *([CFWS] ";" [CFWS] la-parameter) [CFWS] CRLF

la-disposition  = "attachment" / extension-token

la-parameter    = cid-param / url-param / size-param /
                  digest-param / filename-param / type-param /
                  key-param / expires-param /
                  other-param

cid-param       = "cid" "=" value
                  ; value is id-left "@" id-right

url-param       = "url" "=" value
                  ; value is an absolute "https" URI

size-param      = "size" "=" 1*DIGIT

digest-param    = "digest-" token "=" value
                  ; token is a hash algorithm name,
                  ; value is base64 with padding

filename-param  = "filename" "=" value

type-param      = "type" "=" value
                  ; value is type "/" subtype

key-param       = "key" "=" value
                  ; value is key-value

expires-param   = "expires" "=" quoted-string
                  ; content is date-time

other-param     = attribute "=" value
~~~

When the value of a parameter is a quoted-string, any folding
whitespace inside it is removed before the value is used.  This allows
a long URL to be folded across lines without {{MIME-PARAM}} encoding.

## Example {#example-header}

~~~
Link-Attachment: attachment;
    cid="a1.20261117T091500Z@mail.example.com";
    filename="site-survey.mp4"; type="video/mp4"; size=734003200;
    url="https://files.example.com/la/
         Hq3Ld9YtW2xKq8vZp0aN5uFbE7cJr1sM";
    digest-sha-256="X48E9qOokqqrvdts8nOJRJN3OWDUoyWxBf7kbu9DBPE=";
    key="aes128gcm:9Mzuq2AvtfQJxwuIzYB4Gg";
    expires="Thu, 17 Dec 2026 09:15:00 +0000"
~~~

# Message Body {#body}

A message with linked attachments MUST also be readable by recipients
whose MUAs don't support this document.  Its body therefore MUST
include, for each linked attachment, a link from which the recipient
can download the file using a web browser, with its file name and size.

This download link is not, in general, the URL in the "url" parameter.
The "url" parameter gives the file as stored, which may be encrypted;
the download link leads to the file as the recipient wants it.  The
attachment server needs the key to decrypt the file, so the download
link SHOULD include the key, in the format of {{key-format}}, for example
as the value of a query parameter.  The attachment server chooses the
structure of its own URLs; including the algorithm name means that it
can change algorithms without changing that structure.  See
{{security}} for the consequences.

A message with linked attachments SHOULD have an HTML {{HTML}} body
part.  A text/plain body part, if present, SHOULD contain the same
links, but text/plain offers no way to mark them, so the MUA can't hide
them.

## HTML Markup {#html}

In the HTML body, the link to each linked attachment, and any text that
describes only that attachment, SHOULD be enclosed in a "div" element
whose "class" attribute contains "link-attachment" and whose "id"
attribute is the value of the "cid" parameter of the matching
Link-Attachment header field.

All of the linked attachments, together with any surrounding text that
introduces them, MAY be enclosed in a "div" element whose "class"
attribute contains "link-attachments".

~~~ html
<div class="link-attachments">
  <p>Bron Gondwana shared a file with you:</p>
  <div class="link-attachment"
       id="a1.20261117T091500Z@mail.example.com">
<a href=
"https://dl.example/pR7wQx?k=aes128gcm:9Mzuq2AvtfQJxwuIzYB4Gg"
>site-survey.mp4</a> (700 MB)
  </div>
</div>
~~~

An MUA that displays a linked attachment in the same way as an attached
file:

* SHOULD hide each "link-attachment" element whose "id" matches the
  "cid" of a linked attachment that it displays; and

* SHOULD hide each "link-attachments" element whose "link-attachment"
  elements it has all hidden.

An MUA MUST NOT hide a "link-attachment" element that doesn't match a
Link-Attachment header field of the message, or whose linked attachment
it can't display, for example because the file couldn't be fetched or
didn't match its digest.  The recipient can then still use the link.

# Sending {#sending}

## Publishing the File {#publishing}

The sending MUA, or its service, stores the file on an attachment
server.  If the file is not meant to be public, the sender SHOULD
encrypt it ({{params}}) with a key generated at random for that file.

The URL in the "url" parameter, and the download link in the body, MUST
NOT be guessable, unless the file is one that the sender has already
made public.  They SHOULD each contain at least 128 bits of
cryptographically random data, as a capability URL {{CAPABILITY-URLS}}.
Possession of the URL then grants access to the file, and possession of
the message grants possession of the URL.  This gives linked attachments
the fourth property in {{intro}}.

The attachment server MUST respond to a GET request for the URL in the
"url" parameter without authentication, cookies, or any other state not
contained in the URL.  It MUST always return the same content for that
URL, for as long as it serves it.

The attachment server SHOULD keep the file available until the time
given in the "expires" parameter, if any, and SHOULD keep it for at least
several days in any case, so that every receiving server has a chance to
fetch it.

The attachment server's download page SHOULD send a Referrer-Policy
header field with the value "no-referrer", so that the URL doesn't leak
in requests to other sites.

## Composing the Message {#composing}

The sending MUA adds a Link-Attachment header field for each linked
attachment, and includes links in the message body ({{body}}).

If the message is signed with DKIM {{DKIM}}, the signature SHOULD cover
all Link-Attachment header fields, and SHOULD prevent the addition of
more, by listing "Link-Attachment" in the "h=" tag once more than the
number of fields present.

A linked attachment can be kept in a draft message, and is then
published only once.

## Forwarding and Replying {#forwarding}

When a user forwards a message that has linked attachments, or replies
to it and keeps its attachments, the MUA SHOULD copy the Link-Attachment
header fields, and the matching parts of the body, into the new
message.  The "cid" stays the same, so that receiving servers that
already hold the file can recognise it.

If the original URL has expired, or may expire before the new
recipients fetch it, the MUA MAY instead publish the file again, from
its own copy, and give a new URL.  Because the digest is unchanged, a
receiving server that already holds the file need not fetch it again.

# Receiving {#receiving}

## Fetching and Verifying {#fetching}

To fetch a linked attachment, a receiving server or MUA makes a GET
request to the URL in the "url" parameter.

* The request MUST NOT include cookies, credentials, or other
  information that identifies the recipient.

* The client MUST verify the server's certificate as described in
  {{Section 4.3.4 of HTTP}}.

* The client MAY follow redirects, to "https" URIs only, and SHOULD
  limit the number of redirects it follows.

* Any final status code other than 2xx means that the fetch failed.  A
  fetch that failed with a 5xx status code, or a network error, MAY be
  retried later, with increasing delays between attempts.

* The client SHOULD stop reading the content as soon as it exceeds the
  size it expects: the value of the "size" parameter, plus, for
  "aes128gcm", the overhead determined from the record size in the
  header of the encrypted content.

The client then decrypts the content, if "key" is present, and checks
that its size matches the "size" parameter and that its digest matches
every "digest-*" parameter whose algorithm the client supports.  If the
client supports none of the algorithms, or any check fails, the linked
attachment is unavailable.  A client MUST NOT present an unavailable
linked attachment to the user as a verified attachment.

A receiving server MAY decline to fetch a linked attachment, for
example because it is larger than the server's policy allows.  It is
then left to the MUA to fetch it, or to the user to follow the link.

## Content Scanning {#scanning}

A receiving server that scans the content of attached files, for example
for malware, SHOULD fetch and scan each linked attachment that it would
have scanned had the same file been attached to the message.  It
applies the same policy to the result as it would to an attachment:
for example, it can reject or quarantine the message.

A receiving server that scans messages before accepting them can fetch
linked attachments during the SMTP transaction, but large files may take
too long.  It MAY instead accept the message and scan the linked
attachments before delivering it to the recipient's mailbox.

## Storage and Access {#storage}

A message store SHOULD fetch each linked attachment when it delivers the
message, if it has not already been fetched during scanning, and SHOULD
store the verified file alongside the message.

The message store SHOULD provide a way for the user's MUAs to fetch the
stored file from the message store rather than from the attachment
server, identified by its digest.  Then the MUA doesn't contact the
attachment server, and the file remains available after the attachment
server has removed it.

A message store MUST NOT let a user fetch a stored file by its digest
unless the user can read a message that refers to that file.  Otherwise
a user who knows a digest could fetch, or learn of the existence of,
files sent to other users.

{:aside}
> Open issue: How this is done is specific to each access protocol.
> For JMAP {{JMAP-MAIL}}, an Email property could list linked
> attachments with a blobId for each stored file, and {{BLOBEXT}}
> already allows blobs to be described by their digests.  For IMAP, a
> FETCH data item would be needed.  These might belong in separate
> documents.

An MUA whose message store doesn't provide stored files MAY fetch the
linked attachment itself ({{fetching}}).  It SHOULD do so when the user
asks to open the file, or in the background when the message arrives,
rather than when the message is displayed ({{privacy}}).

## Lifetime {#lifetime}

A message store that stores a linked attachment SHOULD keep it for as
long as it keeps any message that refers to it, and MAY remove it once
no such message remains.  A message store can keep a single copy of a
file that many messages refer to, using its digest to find duplicates.

This gives a linked attachment the same lifetime as an attached file:
it lasts as long as the recipient's message does, whatever happens to
the attachment server.

# Requirements and Alternatives Not Taken {#alternatives}

This design meets the following requirements:

* MUAs that don't support this document can show links to the files.

* A message can have many linked attachments.

* Linked attachments can be kept in drafts, forwarded and replied to.

* Receiving systems can restore the properties in {{intro}}.

The message/external-body media type {{MEDIA-TYPES}} also refers to
external content, but many widely deployed MUAs display it as an
attachment with no visible link, and it has neither a digest nor a
key.

{{BIG-FILES-00}} placed a header field in the header section of the
text/plain or text/html body part that held the fallback links.  This
document places it in the message header section instead, where
receiving servers can find it without parsing the MIME structure, and
where it is covered by a DKIM signature in the same way as other header
fields.

# IANA Considerations {#iana}

## Message Header Field {#iana-header}

IANA is asked to add the following entry to the "Permanent Message
Header Field Names" registry, following the procedures of {{REG-PROC}}:

Header field name:
: Link-Attachment

Applicable protocol:
: mail

Status:
: standard

Author/change controller:
: IETF

Specification document(s):
: this document

## Link-Attachment Parameters {#iana-params}

IANA is asked to create a "Link-Attachment Parameters" registry.  New
entries are added under the Specification Required policy.  Each entry
has a parameter name, a description, and a reference.  The initial
entries are:

| Name | Description | Reference |
|---|---|---|
| cid | Identifier of the linked attachment | {{params}} |
| url | URL from which the file can be fetched | {{params}} |
| size | Size of the file in octets | {{params}} |
| digest-* | Digest of the file, by hash algorithm | {{params}} |
| filename | Suggested file name | {{params}} |
| type | Media type of the file | {{params}} |
| key | Encryption algorithm and key | {{params}} |
| expires | Time until which the file is intended to be available | {{params}} |

# Security Considerations {#security}

**Access to the file.**  Anyone who has the message can fetch the file,
because the message contains the URL and the key.  This is deliberate:
it is how attached files behave.  But a URL can leak more easily than
an attachment, for example through browser history, server logs or a
Referer header field.  Encrypting the file means that a leaked URL alone
doesn't reveal it.  The download link in the body ({{body}}) contains the
key, so it is as sensitive as the message itself, and the attachment
server sees the key when it is used.  A sender that doesn't want the
attachment server to be able to read the file can omit the key from the
download link, at the cost of recipients without aware MUAs being unable
to use it.  An attachment server could instead put the key in the
fragment of the download link and decrypt the file in the browser.

**Guessable URLs.**  A guessable URL ({{publishing}}) lets anyone fetch
the file without the message.  Encryption limits the damage, but the
URL also reveals that the file exists, and its size.

**Changed content.**  The digest lets a receiver detect a file that was
changed after the message was sent, whether by the sender, the
attachment server or an attacker.  The digest is only as trustworthy as
the header field it is in; a DKIM signature that covers the
Link-Attachment header field ({{composing}}) binds it to the signing
domain.

**Server-side request forgery.**  A receiving server that fetches URLs
taken from messages can be made to send requests to arbitrary hosts.
Receiving servers MUST NOT fetch a linked attachment from an address
that is not publicly routable, such as a loopback, link-local or
private address, or from a host that resolves to one, and MUST apply
the same check after each redirect.

**Resource use.**  A message can name many large files, or files that
are larger than "size" claims.  Receiving servers SHOULD limit the
number and total size of the linked attachments they fetch for a
message, and stop reading content that exceeds the expected size
({{fetching}}).

**Misleading links.**  The links in the body might not match the
Link-Attachment header fields.  An MUA that hides the links ({{html}})
and displays the verified file instead protects the user from this.
MUAs that don't support this document show the links as written, as
they would any other link.

**Scanning encrypted files.**  The key is in the message, so a
receiving server that can read the message can decrypt and scan the
file.  The encryption protects the file from the attachment server and
from anyone who obtains the URL alone, not from systems that handle the
message.

**Digest lookups.**  A message store that serves files by digest MUST
check that the user can read a message that refers to the file
({{storage}}).

# Privacy Considerations {#privacy}

The attachment server sees every request for the file.  If an MUA
fetches the file when the message is displayed, the request tells the
sender when the recipient read the message, and from where, much as a
remote image does.  A sender could also give each recipient a different
URL to learn which of them opened it.

Fetching by the receiving server when the message arrives ({{scanning}},
{{storage}}) reveals only that the message was delivered, and the
request comes from the receiving server rather than the recipient's
device.  MUAs SHOULD fetch linked attachments from their message store
where possible, and otherwise SHOULD NOT fetch them merely because a
message was displayed.

